{
  "summary": "Adds strict numeric bounds and range checking for command-line and configuration file options across node initialization, chainstate management, mempool limits, network buffers, and wallet settings. Numeric options that previously parsed signed integers without validation now reject negative values, zero where invalid, or values that would overflow narrower internal types. It also standardizes the `=<n>` value placeholder in `-help` messages for these options.",
  "problem": "Numeric arguments were parsed into signed integer types without checking whether values fit the underlying destination types or logic constraints. Negative or excessively large values could silently wrap into massive unsigned integers, overflow during scaling calculations, or invert time offsets, causing nodes to disable database flushing during reindex (`-dbbatchsize`), become permanently stuck in IBD (`-maxtipage`), allow unbounded `OP_RETURN` sizes (`-datacarriersize`), or silently force wallet transactions onto fallback fees (`-txconfirmtarget`).",
  "evidence": [
    "Author notes in commit message: 'A negative -dbbatchsize wrapped to a huge value and disabled batch flushing, so a reindex could hold an unbounded batch in memory.'",
    "Source code comment in src/wallet/init.cpp: 'Fee estimation rejects targets outside the range it tracks, which makes every transaction silently fall back to -fallbackfee, so also reject targets above the estimator's maximum.'"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "Touches startup argument handling across multiple independent subsystems, including node coins view options, mempool limits, chainstate options, wallet configuration, and network buffers.",
  "changed_since_previous": "Refined error handling and unified rejection messages for out-of-range integers and forbidden argument negations across test cases.",
  "card": "Validates ranges and bounds for numeric configuration arguments across the node, mempool, networking, and wallet subsystems. Previously, passing negative or out-of-range values could wrap into unsigned integers or invert time offsets, silently breaking runtime behavior such as disabling database cache flushes during reindex, staying permanently in initial block download, or defaulting wallet transactions to fallback fees. The PR causes invalid numeric arguments to fail startup with explicit error messages and updates help text placeholders.",
